+---
+node: previews
+---
+
+# Decision: Preview Cache Eviction Policy (2Q)
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker serves a bimodal workload: long sequential screenings that request each sheet once, and comparison sessions that re-request a small working set repeatedly. The cache is a private 12 GiB JPEG store inside the worker; a miss costs one ~450 ms render and is ordinary behavior, not a failure. Every candidate policy keeps the 12 GiB bound and the fixed 200/202/404 HTTP contract — eviction is internal bookkeeping — so those are background invariants here, not discriminators. All replacement bookkeeping stays private to the worker (HLD tenet), which keeps the cost of any policy's metadata contained.
+
+What is at stake if the policy is wrong: either comparison sheets churn during screenings (editors then wait on re-renders mid-review), or the worker carries heavy bookkeeping and keeps serving yesterday's favorites after the team moves to a new production.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scan resistance (major)** — a long sequential screening (thousands of one-use sheets) must not evict the comparison working set. The workload alternates between the two shapes, so the policy has to hold under their interleaving.
+- **Adaptation on project switch (major)** — when editors move to a new production, the cache should favor the new working set promptly, without manual clearing or per-project calibration.
+- **Bookkeeping cost (moderate)** — per-request work and per-entry metadata should stay modest; the privacy tenet contains the cost either way, but simpler mechanisms are easier to verify.
+- **Tuning burden (moderate)** — parameters that need per-workload calibration add pilot-phase operational work; parameter-free policies are preferable at equal fit.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU — single recency list
+
+Every entry lives on one recency list; each hit moves the entry to the most-recent end, and eviction removes the least-recent end.
+
+- Scan resistance: **weak** — a sequential screening sweeps every new sheet through the list and evicts the comparison set, regardless of how often it was used before.
+- Adaptation on project switch: **strong** — new work immediately dominates the list.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong** — one list, one move per hit.
+- Tuning burden: **strong** — no parameters.
+
+Summary: the least bookkeeping and the fastest adaptation, at the cost of the protection this workload most needs.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+Each entry carries a hit counter decayed by a periodic aging pass; eviction removes the lowest-count entries. Repeatedly popular sheets accumulate high counts and survive.
+
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — one-use screening sheets stay at low counts while comparison sheets accumulate.
+- Adaptation on project switch: **partial** — aging eventually damps old favorites, but decay constants trade directly against retention; a lag is inherent.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak** — per-entry counters plus a periodic aging pass over the whole cache.
+- Tuning burden: **weak** — aging rate and eviction thresholds need per-workload calibration.
+
+Summary: good retention, paid for in maintenance and slow adaptation to workload shifts.
+
+### Two-queue (2Q) — first-use queue plus reuse queue
+
+Newly admitted sheets enter a FIFO first-use queue bounded to a share of the budget; a sheet's second reference promotes it to a reuse queue managed by reuse recency. Screening bursts churn the first-use queue only. No counters, no periodic passes, no retained history for evicted keys.
+
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — one-off screening sheets never reach the reuse queue, so comparison sheets survive screenings of any length.
+- Adaptation on project switch: **strong** — a new working set promotes on its own second references, and stale entries fall out by reuse recency with no aging constants.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial** — two lists and a promotion move, but no counters and no periodic passes.
+- Tuning burden: **partial** — one parameter, the queue split, calibrated once with pilot hit-rate data.
+
+Summary: matches frequency-with-aging's retention and LRU's adaptation, at constant, moderate bookkeeping.
+
+## Selection
+
+Two-queue (2Q). The workload is bimodal, so scan resistance — the criterion the simplest option fails — is decisive: LRU's advantage is bookkeeping, which the privacy tenet already contains inside the worker, while its weakness is paid by editors directly, mid-comparison. 2Q matches frequency-with-aging on retention without its counter maintenance, and beats it on project switches by adapting through reuse recency instead of aging constants. The "a cache miss is ordinary behavior" tenet also turns in 2Q's favor: the policy's one real weakness — a hot sheet evicted from the reuse queue re-renders once — is an ordinary ~450 ms miss, not a failure. Committing obligates calibrating the queue split with pilot hit-rate data (default 25% first-use / 75% reuse) and keeping the promotion bookkeeping private. It forecloses nothing structural: the HTTP contract and the 12 GiB bound are policy-invariant.
