 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately, removed on completion or failure, and does not count against either queue's share. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Two-Queue Replacement
+
+The cache uses two queues with fixed shares of the 12 GiB budget:
+
+- **First-use queue** — newly admitted sheets that have not been requested since admission. Managed oldest-first. Its share is configurable, defaulting to one quarter of the budget (3 GiB).
+- **Reuse queue** — sheets that have been requested again after admission. Managed least-recently-reused first. Holds the remainder of the budget (9 GiB at the default split).
+
+Placement and movement:
+
+1. A completed sheet is admitted to the first-use queue, evicting that queue's oldest entries as needed to stay within its share.
+2. A request served as 200 from a first-use-queue sheet promotes the sheet to the reuse queue, evicting the reuse queue's least-recently-reused entries as needed. A request answered with 202 while a job is pending does not affect queue placement.
+3. A request served as 200 from a reuse-queue sheet marks it most recently reused.
+4. Eviction discards a key's history: a later request for the same sheet is a first use, served through the ordinary miss path. No ghost lists or reference counts are retained.
+
+Queue movement is internal bookkeeping and never changes the response a request receives. A long sequential screening churns first-use entries while comparison sheets remain in the reuse queue; the queue split is the only tuning parameter.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | Two-queue (2Q) replacement — see `decisions/eviction-policy.md` | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU evicts comparison sheets during long sequential screenings. Aged frequency counts add counter maintenance and keep yesterday's favorites warm after an editor switches projects. 2Q's extra cost — two queues and a tunable split — stays inside this worker's private bookkeeping. |
+| 2Q shape | Two queues only (first-use FIFO, reuse by recency) | Classic 2Q with A1OUT/A2OUT ghost directories | Ghost lists would save a single ~450 ms render for a sheet re-requested after eviction; a cache miss is ordinary behavior, so that saving does not justify permanent directory bookkeeping. |
+| Queue bounds | Fixed per-queue shares of the 12 GiB budget, measured in JPEG bytes | Soft/borrowing split between queues; entry-count bounds | Fixed shares keep the global bound (PREVIEW-004) structural — the shares sum to 12 GiB — and eviction behavior predictable. Sheets are uniformly ~2 MiB, so byte and count bounds behave alike; bytes match the budget's unit. |
+| Split default | First-use 25% / reuse 75% | 50/50; a smaller first-use share | The reuse queue holds the protected comparison working set and deserves most of the budget; 3 GiB (about 1,500 sheets) absorbs a screening burst in the first-use queue. To be recalibrated with pilot hit-rate data. |
+| Reuse-queue eviction order | Least-recently-reused first | FIFO by promotion time | Among sheets proven reused, the least recently reused is the least likely to return. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Calibrate the first-use/reuse split (default 25%/75%) against pilot hit-rate data per production workload.
